  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThe surface cold front has made it into the northern Gulf Coast \nstates with expansive surface high pressure over the Great Lakes \nregion. Low clouds and patchy drizzle have cleared somewhat across \nour northern half of the CWA, however may still have a few lingering \npatches of drizzle across southern KY this morning where low clouds \nare hanging in. Otherwise, look for a dry day with a steady NE \nsurface wind and variable cloudiness depending on your location. \nSouthern IN and northern KY are more likely to see mostly clear \nskies later today, while central and southern KY have a better shot \nat more cloud cover. Decided to stick with highs on the lower side \nof temperature guidance given the fairly steady NE surface winds \nreinforcing cooler air over the region. The better chance at \nsunshine across the northern CWA will help offset this a bit, but \nstill looking for low and mid 70s in most spots. It is possible some \nlocations don't get out of the 60s.\n\nWill carry some scattered shower chances across southern KY into the \nLake Cumberland region later tonight into Sunday as a shortwave \nmoves through the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley region. As this \noccurs, it should be able to tap into the northern fringes over the \ndeeper moisture shield stretching from the western Gulf up through \nthe mid Atlantic. Overall QPF amounts for our southern counties will \nbe very light. Otherwise, look for dry conditions across the rest of \nthe CWA with temperatures reaching the mid 70s. Still will have a \nbit of N/NE surface flow which could hurt temp recovery a bit. \nLeaned on the lower side of guidance here as well.\n\nSurface high pressure and unremarkable NW flow aloft take back hold \nof the area going into Sunday night. Pretty good radiational cooling \nconditions expected along with mostly clear skies will allow for \ntemps dropping into the mid 40s to low 50s across the area. \n\nThe upper-level low swings directly to our north Monday into \nTuesday, flattening out our UL trough into a westerly flow aloft. \nThis should swing our gusty surface winds around from northeasterly \nto easterly by the early Tuesday morning hours. We should stay \ncooler on Monday and Tuesday as we aren't advecting warmer air from \nour south or west, with highs in the lower 70s. Lows remain in the \nlower 40s for the majority; however, isolated upper 30s are \npossible, especially in a couple of cooler valleys across the CWA. \nTuesday keeps a west to east flow aloft that tracks down to the \nsurface throughout the day, and winds should weaken as they undergo \nthe change from easterly to westerly by early Wednesday morning. \nExpect warmer highs on Wednesday and Thursday in the low 80s, with \novernight lows in the the upper 40s and low 50s, with the metro \nstruggling to decouple as much as the rest of the area and therefore \nremaining warmer than the rest of the CWA. Saturday evening and \nSunday bring our next chance of rain showers as a tropical moisture \npushes its way north; expect warm and humid highs in the low to mid \n80s with overnight lows in the upper 50s and cloud cover before \nshowers begin on Sunday morning.",
